%[text] %[text:anchor:T_9c13] # Arrays
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] Both doubles and strings are *scalar* data structures in MATLAB because they represent single values. Many programs require more than a single value to work with. This lead programmers to the concept of an array as a collection of indexed values. A vector of data might track something like the temperature at a weather station over the course of time. A matrix could include columns for each tracked value of temperature, humidity, precipitation, and barometric pressure at each time. If you wish to track several values over time at multiple weather stations, then you would require a 3-D array indexed by the time, the weather variable, and the weather station identifier. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Human thinking "Mouse"](text:image:7247) ![Computer with a picture of a mouse on the screen that is thinking "11010001110100100111110100000111..."](text:image:6b8e)
%[text] This script will introduce vectors, matrices, and 3-dimensional arrays.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Before you get started:**
%[text] This live script is intended to be used with the code visible and output inline. On **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Output Inline**. Alternately, select **Output inline** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:9343) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Idea symbol](text:image:76fe) Interacting with this live script will build some familiarity with MATLAB, as concepts and commands are introduced together, but some familiarity with the Live Editor environment was introduced in [IntroductionSoln.m](file:IntroductionSoln.m). 
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:6739)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one, including running all code blocks.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5697] ## Compound Data Structures
%[text]  
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0daf] ### Vectors
%[text] A *vector* of values is a one-dimensional indexed list of elements that may be arranged either horizontally (a row vector) such as the example
% run this code
DaysOfWeek = ["Monday" "Tuesday" "Wednesday" "Thursday" "Friday" "Saturday" "Sunday"]
%[text] or vertically (a column vector) such as the example
Ordinals = ["first";"second";"third";"fourth";"fifth"]
%%
%[text] Notice that each vector is labeled with a pair of numbers. The row vector is called a `1x7 string` because it has 1 row and 7 columns, each containing a [string element](file:DataSoln.m). The column vector is called a `5x1 string` because it contains 5 rows and 1 column, each containing a string element. In general, each element in a vector must be the same type of data. We can make a column vector containing [doubles](file:DataSoln.m):
Nums = [6;7]
%[text] and even concatenate vectors together along a matching dimension:
NewVector = [Ordinals;Nums]
%[text] But if the programming language allows this to happen, it will enforce a common type for every element of the result. In the example of `NewVector` that means the elements that had been doubles in `nums` become strings in `NewVector`.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0f5f] #### Indexing
%[text] Recall that a vector was identified as a one-dimensional *indexed* list of elements. This means that, as a programmer, you can access a single element (or range of elements) in a vector by identifying them by index (that is by number) in the list.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A 1x5 row vector with reference indices labeled for each (row,column): (1,1) then (1,2) then (1,3) then (1,4) then (1,5)](text:image:6bca)
%[text] Programming languages differ in their indexing, but they either start indexing elements with 1 (such as MATLAB) or 0 (such as Python). Since there is only one changing index in a vector (either the column index for row vectors or the row index for column vectors) that single index is sufficient to uniquely identify the entry. 
% run this code
DaysOfWeek(5)
Ordinals(2:4)
k = 3;
"The " + Ordinals(k) + " day of the week is " + DaysOfWeek(k) + "."
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_09D8BED5] ![](text:image:1f60) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. Run the code below. 
%[text] 2. Then run the code without transposing `DaysOfWeek`. What is different? 
%[text] 3. What does the `transpose()` function do? How can you figure it out?
%[text] 4. What happens if you run the command to set the value of `allDays` with `NewVector` instead of `Ordinals`? Or with `DaysOfWeek` instead of `DaysOfWeek(1:5)`? Or both?  \
AllDays = "The " + Ordinals + " day of the week is " + transpose(DaysOfWeek(1:5)) + "."
%[text] ![](text:image:917d) **Pro-tip**. Another way to find the transpose of a matrix `M` in MATLAB is to write `M'`.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7b16] #### Vector Addition
%[text] Vector addition requires that the vectors in question be of the same dimension. If the dimensions are identical, the operation proceeds term-wise with each element being added to the element with the same index in the other matrix. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A pictorial representation of vector addition adding two row vectors (heart on green, heart on red, heart on blue, heart on yellow, heart on purple) to (swirl on green, swirl on red, swirl on yellow, swirl on purple) to get (heart and swirl on green, heart and swirl on red, heart and swirl on blue, heart and swirl on yellow, heart and swirl on purple)](text:image:74ca)
%[text] As in the case of concatenation of `string` types and `double` types resulting in `string` values as output, MATLAB is designed to change your data structures if it is necessary to execute a command. In the case of
AllDays = "The " + Ordinals + " day of the week is " + transpose(DaysOfWeek(1:5)) + "."
%[text] it is necessary to expand the `1x1` vectors `"The"` and `" day of the week is "` and "." to be `5x1` vectors, just like `Ordinals` and `DaysOfWeek(1:5)` as shown:
%[text]{"align":"center"} !["The" becomes a 5x1 vector of "The"s added to ordinal, a 5x1 vector containing "first", "second", etc. which is added to a 5x1 vector of copies of "day of the week is" which is added to the 5x1 vector dayOfWeek, "Monday", "Tuesday", etc which is added to a 5x1 vector of "."](text:image:6a68)
%[text] Concatenating each element with its corresponding index values (in this case, matching color) in the other vectors, the result is a new `5x1` vector of strings.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A 5x1 vector of sentences. The (1,1) entry is "The first day of the week is Monday." the (2,1) entry is "The second day of the week is Tuesday.", etc.](text:image:8021)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_93012E6B] ![](text:image:2df7) **Reflect**.
%[text] 1. Vector operations can be extremely effective, but you can also accidentally perform a very different operation than you intended. How can you check whether you have, in fact, performed the operation you meant? 
%[text] 2. What do you think will happen if you perform other mathematical operations involving numeric vectors? Are there any standard patterns to what works as you expect and what does not? \
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_E753333E] **Example: Mathematical operations on numerical vectors**
vec1 = [1 2 3 4 5] %[output:8e8e0c0b]
vec2 = 10*vec1 %[output:50cdbf59]
vec3 = 10 + vec1 %[output:966efe60]
vec4 = vec1 + vec1 %[output:64be7c13]
% Generates error because of dimensional mismatch
try %[output:group:0e9eb331]
vec5a = vec1*vec1
catch ME
    disp(ME.message) %[output:258802b3]
end %[output:group:0e9eb331]
vec5b = vec1.*vec1 %[output:5dd4ba01]
vec6 = vec1*transpose(vec1) %[output:1e84c642]
vec7 = transpose(vec1)*vec1 %[output:63e6a71a]
vec8 = sqrt(vec1) %[output:23edf0fa]
% Generates error because of dimensional mismatch
try %[output:group:7af8997f]
    vec9 = vec1^2
catch ME
    disp(ME.message) %[output:391dfcba]
end %[output:group:7af8997f]
vec10 = vec1.^2 %[output:903064b0]
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_77010E35] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] % Copy and paste these for testing in the code box above
%[text] % Remember that if you encounter an error you must fix the error in order to proceed
%[text] % with the rest of the code
%[text] vec2 = 10*vec1
%[text] vec3 = 10 + vec1
%[text] vec4 = vec1 + vec1
%[text] vec5a = vec1*vec1
%[text] vec5b = vec1.*vec1
%[text] vec6 = vec1*transpose(vec1)
%[text] vec7 = transpose(vec1)*vec1
%[text] vec8 = sqrt(vec1)
%[text] vec9 = vec1^2
%[text] vec10 = vec1.^2
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_45e5] ### Matrices
%[text] A matrix is a 2-dimensional collection of $mn${"altText":"mn"} elements arranged in `m` rows and `n` columns, such that the `(i,j)`th element is in the `i`th row and `j`th column.  
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A 4x5 matrix with 4 rows and 5 columns. There are reference indices for each entry in the form (row,column) such that (3,2) is the cell in the third row and second column](text:image:231e)
%[text] While every vector is trivially a matrix, the result of `vec7` in the Reflection [examples above](internal:M_77010E35) is a more standard `5x5` matrix. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_56ac] #### Matrix Multiplication
%[text] As you saw in the [Example](internal:M_E753333E) above, vector multiplication can sometimes result in a significantly larger matrix as output. This is because the `i`th row of the first matrix has to be multiplied term-wise with the `j`th column of the second matrix and then those values are summed to determine the value in the `(i,j)`th position in the output matrix. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![To multiply a 3x5 matrix by a 5x2 matrix, the (i,j)th element of the result is the sum of the  (i,1)th element times the (1,j)th element and the (i,2)th element times the (2,j)th element](text:image:6b4e)
%[text] ![](text:image:469e) **Exercise 1**.
%[text] Use the diagram of matrix multiplication to explain the dimensions and values in the computed result
vec1 = [1 2 3 4 5];
vec7 = transpose(vec1)*vec1
%[text] 
%[text] **Note:** One novel property of matrix multiplication is that it is NOT commutative. In the figure above, there is a `3x5` matrix multiplied by a `5x2` matrix to result in a `3x2` matrix. If we try to compute a `5x2` matrix multiplied by a `3x5` matrix, we can't even get started because the top row (sailboat, X) doesn't contain the same number of elements as the first column (heart, swirl, star) and so cannot possibly be multiplied term-wise.
%%
