%[text] Start with the matrix
ex3 = [1 2 3; 5 6 7]
%[text]     a) Change the element `6` to be `13`. This means you should have `ex5 =` $\\left\[\\matrix{ 1 & 2 & 3 \\cr 5 & 13 & 7 }\\right\]${"altText":"matrix with rows \[1, 2, 3\] and \[5, 13, 7\]"}.
%[text]     b) Extract just the first row of the matrix and call it `row1ex5`. Thus, you should have `row1ex5 =` $\\left\[\\matrix{1 & 2 & 3}\\right\]${"altText":"row vector \[1, 2, 3\]"}.
%[text]     c) Append another row to create the matrix `ex5 =` $\\left\[\\matrix{1 & 2 & 3 \\cr 5 & 13 & 7 \\cr 4 & 8 & 12}\\right\]${"altText":"matrix with rows \[1, 2, 3\], \[5, 13, 7\], and \[4, 8, 12\]"}.
%[text] **Exercise 3 Solution**
ex3a = [1 2 3;5 13 7]
row1ex3 = ex3(1,:)
ex3c = [ex3;4 8 12]
