%[text]     b) Starting from the blank matrix below, use indexing to recreate the letter shown in [`SimpleIm0`](internal:M_0B41680B).
blank = zeros(20);

% % Solution
% Top line
blank(4:5,4:17) = ones([2,14]);
% Bottom line
blank(16:17,4:17) = ones([2,14]);
% Middle line
blank(10:11,6:11) = ones([2,6]);
% Remainder of vertical spine
blank(6:15,4:5) = ones([10,2]);

imshow(blank,"InitialMagnification",1000)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9fa7] #### Standard File Types
%[text] You have probably encountered various image file types. For the rest of this script, you will be investigating a color photograph of turkeys that was saved in the standard image format of JPEG.
% Load an image of turkeys %[text:anchor:H_7AF4BD41]
PicTurkeys = imread("turkeys1.jpg");
% Split the RGB image into three channels each given by a matrix
% representing hue, saturation and value.  
hsvTurkeys = rgb2hsv(PicTurkeys);
[h,s,v] = imsplit(hsvTurkeys);
montage([h;s;v])
%[text] Notice that `montage` has created a matrix of images! Not every matrix is so nice to visualize.
%[text] ![](text:image:529a) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. In the argument to `montage()`, the matrices are concatenated vertically. What happens if you concatenate them horizontally?
%[text] 2. The original `PicTurkeys` is an RGB image which stores values in terms of the amount of red, green, and blue required to display the complete image. If you use `imsplit()` on `picTurkeys` (rather than on `hsvTurkeys`) with output `[redChannel,greenChannel,blueChannel]` and then use `montage()` to display the result, do you get what you expect?  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_74e4] ### Higher Dimensional Arrays
%[text] RGB images are 3-dimensional arrays, as they are made up of three 2-dimensional matrices. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![An array represented as three 4x5 matrices where the front one is red, the middle one is green and the rear one is blue. The depth is tracked by a third parameter (row, column, channel)](text:image:9fa2)
%[text] If you build a 3D array with the other channels being entirely `0`'s, you will be able to generate the expected results for `redChannel, greenChannel,` and `blueChannel` images.
%[text] Example:
[RedChannel,GreenChannel,BlueChannel] = imsplit(PicTurkeys);
NoData = zeros(size(RedChannel));
OnlyRed = cat(3,RedChannel,NoData,NoData);
imshow(OnlyRed)
%[text] ![](text:image:1cb6) **Exercise 5**.
%[text]         a. Copy and modify the example code to display only the green channel `OnlyGreen` and the blue channel `OnlyBlue`.
%[text]         b. Check that your results are correct by running
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] imshow(OnlyRed + OnlyGreen + OnlyBlue)
%[text] ```
%[text]         c. Invert the green channel by replacing `GreenChannel` in the definition of `OnlyGreen` with `255-GreenChannel` since the values for each color range from `0` to `255`. Then display your result.
%[text]  **Exercise 5 Solution**
% % Solution to Exercise 5
OnlyGreen = cat(3,NoData,GreenChannel,NoData);
OnlyBlue = cat(3,NoData,NoData,BlueChannel);
imshow(OnlyGreen)
imshow(OnlyBlue)
imshow(OnlyGreen+OnlyRed+OnlyBlue)
InvGreen = cat(3,NoData,255-GreenChannel,NoData);
imshow(InvGreen)
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:06f7) **Reflect**. 
%[text] 1. What constraints are there on structuring your data as a vector? a matrix? any other type of array?
%[text] 2. What are the benefits of structuring your data as a vector, matrix, or other array?
%[text] 3. Can you think of any reasons to avoid structuring your data as an array?
%[text] 4. What other structures would you like to use? Can you give any examples? \
